# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 usual.cc _lcm: lcm(a,b) (gen.cc) is normal(a/gcd(a,b))*b, which keeps the sign of the product, so the lcm of integers of opposite signs was negative: lcm(-4,6) = -12, lcm(4,-6) = -12, lcm([-4,6,10]) = -60, lcm(-3,-5,-7) = -105, lcm(-2^70,3) = -3541774862152233910272, element-wise lcm([-4,6],[6,4]) = [-12,12], and lcm(-4) = -4 (right 12, 12, 60, 105, 3541774862152233910272, [12,12], 4: the least positive common multiple, as Python math.lcm). Fractions of integers the same: lcm(1/2,-1/3) = -1, lcm(-2/3,4/5) = -4, lcm([-1/2,1/3,1/4]) = -1, lcm(-1/2) = -1/2, lcm(-4,1/3) = -4, element-wise lcm([-1/2,1/3],[1/4,-1/6]) = [-1/2,-1/3] (right 1, 4, 1, 1/2, 4, [1/2,1/3]: lcm(a/b,c/d) = lcm(a,c)/gcd(b,d), positive). gcd is positive already. Now _lcm returns the abs when every argument is an integer (_INT_ or _ZINT) or a fraction whose numerator and denominator are such integers, and the element-wise branch does the same per pair; lcm(a,b) itself, which Giac calls inside, is unchanged, and so are polynomials (lcm(x^2-1,x+1)), floats (lcm(-6,4.0) = -12.0), Gaussian integers and mixed symbolic arguments.
